cab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122406 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122406 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122406 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "199df1de-8548-5048-b04c-6b828a95aeb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122407 #edb/ref 1080 "None of the statements are true" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122407 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122407 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7f4cf1d8-593b-5fe3-9148-dc675c4f85df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122612 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122612 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122612 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122612 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122612 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122612 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122612 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122612 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186122616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122612 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8e8895a9-c9e9-5618-ac22-04862d99cf8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122613 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122613 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122613 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cf3f30e4-0f89-5940-99e4-dcf3910c0fbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122614 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122614 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122614 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d5288fa3-1db8-5e79-92c4-7e99c244d05c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122615 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122615 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122615 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "24437291-99fb-588b-8dab-62cb55817f16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122616 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122616 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122616 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2a686b7e-36e0-5679-9b2e-2caae66b890c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122617 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122617 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122617 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "61daf739-1f7c-576c-9452-8ad79a827c5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124302 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124302 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ![](images/e9/e98eb00ac7ee6ffb83d4a902d92e9bda4434e6bfaeae1fb91a210849d3e1991f.svg) is a linearly independent set in $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n\nII. $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ![](images/71/716877f60112610eae19b094ca691edf0c0d2ecdffc54dc0b785d66c96e64909.svg)\n\nIII. ![](images/cc/ccd021603e72ceb60be92eabec243bfdd5ce33eb22a6b801030a05efc02a861d.svg) is a basis of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124302 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4b1227cb-7cf9-534d-a22f-e7acf7df9dc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124302 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n![](images/a1/a16ee652347a67e66994875711dff835418bfcfb4f85a058a80f9164af525cab.svg)\nis true if and only if $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n![](images/f1/f173928232bbfd7680c2952250c78e7ba354bd4851cb4783bd531cc683044db5.svg)\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n![](images/74/7449707a6aa218f65cc22db3a56e8ed9391e3b5e562301e77fd62cb2f3dad20a.svg)\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry $a_{11} = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/b9/b952b253393b4d14572b41ad1ca265e3b858edeec36e4c937f644621ae497294.svg)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124303 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4831" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124303 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2},- \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$ is a basis of $V$\n\nII. $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}$ spans $V$\n\nIII. $\\left\\{  -\\mathbf{b_2}, 3\\mathbf{b_1}\\right\\}$ is linearly dependent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124303 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "91989c25-1d0d-5639-b3e1-7f7479322b7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124303 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $\\mathcal{B}$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{2}+{x}_{2}(-{b}_{1})+{x}_{3}({b}_{3}+{b}_{1}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{2}+({x}_{3}-{x}_{2}){b}_{1}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n![](images/62/625e812c25818cec68c6a5bcd59a39945feb09526607244e42059ed8af6f9b08.svg)\nAlso, $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2},- \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $\\mathcal{B}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2},- \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{b}_1+x_2\\mathbf{b}_2+x_3\\mathbf{b}_3\n=\nx_2\\mathbf{b}_2+(x_3-x_1)(-\\mathbf{b}_1)+x_3 (\\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1})\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. In order for $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}$ to span $V,$ every element of $\\mathcal{B}$ must be able to be expressed as a linear combination of elements of $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}.$ But the vector $\\mathbf{b}_1 \\in \\mathcal{B}$ can't be written as a linear combination of the elements of $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If $\\left\\{  -\\mathbf{b_2}, 3\\mathbf{b_1}\\right\\}$ is linearly dependent then there exist $x_1$ and $x_2$ not both zero such that\n\n$$\nx_1(-\\mathbf{b_2}) + x_2(3\\mathbf{b_1}) = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nThen, we would have\n\n$$\n(3x_2) \\cdot \\mathbf{b_1} + (-x_1) \\cdot \\mathbf{b_2} + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{b}_3= \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nBut this would mean that $\\mathcal{B} = \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent, which contradicts the fact that $\\mathcal{B}$ is a basis of $V.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124403 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8269" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124403 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\big\\{ 0,t^2 \\big\\}$ is a linearly independent set in $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$\n\nII. $-5t^2$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\big\\{ 0, t^2 \\big\\}$\n\nIII. $\\big\\{ 0, t^2 \\big\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124403 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "92adb651-e2db-5296-817d-aea249afe2b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124403 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1 \\cdot {\\color{blue}0} + 0 \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2} = 0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n-5t^2 = 0 \\cdot {\\color{blue}0} + (-5) \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124489 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124489 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124489 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124489 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124489 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124489 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124489 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124489 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124489 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124489 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124489 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124489 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124489 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124489 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124489 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124489 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124489 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124489 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124489 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" #edb/re