ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124592 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124592 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186447056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124592 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124592 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124592 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Polynomial Vector Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124592 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124592 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2588e089-4786-5346-b42d-65c2740ae2ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124709 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124709 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124709 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124709 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124709 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124709 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124709 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124709 #edb/ref 1045 "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124709 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124709 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124709 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124709 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4535a3e9-8919-5e95-ab78-1b3e6bc5fe1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124966 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124966 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124966 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124966 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{0,t,{t}^{2}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{2}[t]$\n\nII. ${t}^{2}-2t$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{0,t,{t}^{2}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{0,t,{t}^{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}_{2}[t]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124966 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "96532d4b-53ae-5b0b-a44e-d0ba6b721786" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124966 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot t+0\\cdot {t}^{2}=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}-2t=0\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot t+1\\cdot {t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124967 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124967 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124967 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124967 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent\n\nII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},w\\}$ is linearly independent\n\nIII. $\\{2{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of $V$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124967 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "24ec3a38-129c-5be9-91d3-dbe8f97d4c69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124967 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $B$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $B$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, if $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent then there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere at least one of $x$'s is not zero. But then \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+0{b}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $B$ is linearly dependent. This would contradict the fact that $B$ is a basis.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $B$ is a basis of $V,$ it is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors. This means that if we add any new vector to the set, it becomes linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2{b}_{1})+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}_{1}){b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\{2{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{2{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}(2{b}_{1})+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125002 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125002 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36571" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125002 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125002 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125002 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "03a15270-f806-567d-89e2-38996d20cbe3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125002 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]=-2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{12}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125350 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125350 #edb/ref 1061 653375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125350 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b18f66cf-141c-5d9b-abc4-5dec05218f63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125350 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125351 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125351 #edb/ref 1061 653379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125351 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c37255e8-78c3-5483-a38d-706de2e319e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125351 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125352 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125352 #edb/ref 1061 653382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125352 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "914841e5-976c-50b0-9edd-a7475085a0ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125352 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125353 #edb/ref 1061 653386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125353 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4e0810ce-4ac6-549b-8bdf-98dce31cb769" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125353 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125355 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125355 #edb/ref 1061 653383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125355 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e09fa444-e077-5819-b997-b79f912a52f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125355 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125527 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125527 #edb/ref 1061 653376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125527 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "df7f4408-7a22-5728-a508-8a07da995a75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125527 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125763 #edb/ref 1064 "A set of vectors \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}\n$$\n\nis called a **basis** of an abstract vector space $V$ if the following two conditions hold:\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ is linearly independent, and\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ spans $V.$\n\nFor example, let's consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb R$ with degree at most $2,$ and its subset \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B} = \\left\\{ {\\color{blue}{1}}, {\\color{blue}{t}}, {\\color{blue}{t^2}} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIs $\\mathcal{B}$ a basis for $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]?$ Let's check our two conditions.\n\n- First, we check for linear independence. Notice that the equation \n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot {\\color{blue}1} + x_2 \\cdot {\\color{blue}t} + x_3 \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2} = 0\n$$\n\n \nis true for all $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\nx_1=x_2=x_3=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\mathcal{B}$ is linearly independent in $\\mathbb{R}_2[t].$\n\n- Next, we check that $\\mathcal B$ spans $\\mathbb{R}_2[t].$ Notice that we can write any polynomial $at^2+bt+c\\in\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ as a linear combination of vectors from $\\mathcal{B} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nat^2+bt+c=c\\cdot{\\color{blue}1} + b\\cdot{\\color{blue}t} + a\\cdot{\\color{blue}t^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathcal B$ spans $\\mathbb{R}_2[t].$\n\nSince both conditions hold, we conclude that $\\mathcal{B} = \\{ {1}, {t}, {t^2} \\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125763 #edb/ref 1065 3210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125763 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1d94c7bd-3777-5331-b633-0e2463092e26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125763 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125764 #edb/ref 1064 "A set of vectors $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ is a basis of a vector space $V$ if $\\mathcal{B}$ is a **maximally linearly independent set in $V$**. In other words, $\\mathcal{B}$ is linearly independent and for any vector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{w}} \\in V,$ the set \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B} \\cup \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{w}} \\} = \\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{w}} \\}\n$$\n\nis linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125764 #edb/ref 1065 3211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125764 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dc0c1b8c-4abf-5ce9-aac8-763330c5a699" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125764 #edb/ref 1067 "An Alternative Definition" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125765 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ denote the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb{R}$ that have degree at most $n.$ Also, let $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ be the vector space of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}.$ Then, we have the following important standard bases.\n\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_1[t]$ is $\\{ 1, \\: t \\}.$\n\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ is $\\{ 1, \\: t, \\: t^2 \\}.$\n\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ is $\\{ 1, \\: t, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^n \\}.$\n\n- The standard basis of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is\n![](images/11/1178fd622498d7466d7bddc4232ecc3cc9a9d9a8a9362766918069ba43110055.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125765 #edb/ref 1065 3212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125765 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fd76fc85-4c43-564f-96ac-8492b69f8ecf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125765 #edb/ref 1067 "Some Important Standard Bases" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360516 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452916 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360516 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452917 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360516 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452918 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360516 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94850" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360516 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360516 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\{0,{t}^{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{3}[t]$\n\n- **II.** $-{t}^{3}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{0,{t}^{3}\\}$\n\n- **III.** $\\{0,{t}^{3}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}_{3}[t]$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360516 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bf9e33e0-00b4-5d7d-a1a7-55573563c2a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360516 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot {t}^{3}=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n-{t}^{3}=0\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot {t}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360523 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452931 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360523 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452932 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360523 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452933 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360523 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94870" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360523 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360523 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\n- **II.** $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\n- **III.** $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360523 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e6efc92-73ea-52ca-ba80-456c38800684" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360523 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{4}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-5)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+8\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the set is linearly independent and for any matrix in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}=a\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+b\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+c\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+d\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186406849 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406849 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406849 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406849 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406849 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406849 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406849 #edb/ref 1045 "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406849 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186406884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406849 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406849 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406849 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "7a9a1a68-93b3-5d77-a9f3-56fa1ac555d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406883 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454780 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186406883 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454781 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186406883 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454782 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186406883 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80631" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406883 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406883 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ are bases of ${R}^{3}$ and ${R}^{2}$, respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${x,{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- **II.** ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- **III.** ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186406883 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b3557614-de80-516a-bb07-7b50ae99a56a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406883 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set ${x,{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-2{b}_{3})+{x}_{2}(3{b}_{1})+{x}_{3}(5{b}_{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2{x}_{1}){b}_{3}+(3{x}_{2}){b}_{1}+(5{x}_{3}){b}_{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}(3{b}_{1})+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5}(5{b}_{2})+(-\\frac{{x}_{3}}{2})(-2{b}_{3})\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$, any vector of ${R}^{2},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186406884 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406884 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "748c2276-dafd-5926-8dff-6ce428e10c5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406884 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186406883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406885 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406885 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aa1dd52d-ebd7-598e-b1e9-f1f6411b6354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406885 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186406880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406886 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406886 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc7cf94a-8206-5fe0-b854-5c6affbe827a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406886 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186406881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406887 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406887 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8519c82-2015-5d4b-ab7d-68f0931d52be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406887 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406888 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f5d3d190-875f-5a88-afc2-f8fcfd0bc6a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406888 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186406882 